                        "version": 0,
                        "content": "The nurse is caring for a client who underwent a thoracentesis to treat pleural effusion. The pleural fluid testing results indicate the pleural fluid is cloudy and confirm the presence of white blood cells (WBCs). Which condition would the nurse suspect?",
                        "subjectId": "ad5ab04c79144c0ea9b83385308c3b1d",
                        "difficultyIds": "11797937e327eb6d913faa80427c566b",
                        "difficulty": "考试范围-Analyzing 分析",
                        "textAnalysis": "Rationale:\nPleural effusion is a sign of an underlying disease process and the type of pleural fluid can provide information about what underlying disease is responsible for the effusion. Pleural fluid is described as transudative or exudative. Transudative pleural fluid is typically a clear, pale yellow fluid due to the lack of protein or cells in the fluid. Congestive heart failure, chronic liver disease such as cirrhosis, or renal disease may be the underlying cause of a transudative pleural effusion. Exudative fluid is cloudy due to the presence of WBCs and is a sign of an inflammatory or infectious process, including malignancy. In this situation, the pleural fluid is exudative since it is cloudy and WBCs are present. Since options 1, 3 and 4 are likely related to transudative, clear pleural fluid, option 2 is the correct answer.",
                        "id": "51f90009a0854a3188392122ff01fab0",
                        "mark": "0",
                        "collectionState": "0"
                    },
                    {
                        "optionList": [
                            {
                                "isRight": "1",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "c6a1ba19b4e544a1be389862c2bf42b5",
                                "content": "“Influenza has a rapid symptom onset, while common cold symptoms appear gradually.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "6042479e41124f4a8b7ddd2d52131e71",
                                "content": "“It is very common to have a fever with the common cold, while a fever with influenza is rare.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "ed11b989ab33425aa444494e7268205f",
                                "content": "“Severe muscle pains and aches are associated with the common cold and are rare with influenza.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "ddcc03d8bbd04c6aab03a78ac01c7cf0",
                                "content": "“There is an annual vaccination for viral rhinitis, which decreases the severity of the common cold.”"
                            }
                        ],
                        "extJson": {
                            "optionList": [
                                {
                                    "isRight": "1",
